WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.660
I'd like to ask each other
about our early experiences.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.170
Sure.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:07.620
Okay.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.370
Why don't you...

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:08.880
...in French?

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.080
What happened to me in French?

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:10.920
All right.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:11.460
Okay.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:12.630
That's a good start.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:14.450
Okay.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.190
I'd like to

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.160
...
yeah, go ahead.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:21.810
Hello?

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:24.570
Okay.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.570
I think we're live.

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:28.635
So we have the two Steves here.

00:00:29.565 --> 00:00:30.045
that's right.

00:00:30.045 --> 00:00:31.065
We have the two Steves.

00:00:31.485 --> 00:00:32.384
We have Dr.

00:00:32.384 --> 00:00:38.505
Steven Krashen, who is to me, the
person who has explained the world,

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:43.215
the sort of snake pit of Pandora's
box of how you learn languages.

00:00:43.215 --> 00:00:46.394
And he's reduced it to something
that's very simple, which con...

00:00:46.754 --> 00:00:49.635
basically confirms all of my
experience in language learning.

00:00:49.905 --> 00:00:50.355
Dr.

00:00:50.355 --> 00:00:55.525
Krashen the language acquisition
hypothesis, we're gonna get into that.

00:00:56.175 --> 00:00:58.335
Very happy to have this
opportunity to speak with you.

00:00:59.535 --> 00:01:03.225
And I'm very happy to be here
with my language consultant

00:01:03.225 --> 00:01:04.994
and therapist, Steve Kaufmann.

00:01:05.175 --> 00:01:06.975
Who's helped me in so many ways.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:07.515
Let me tell you.

00:01:07.695 --> 00:01:11.385
Well, that's very kind of you, you
know, you were saying just before

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.555
we went live here, that it would
be interesting to review our early

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.505
experiences in learning languages.

00:01:17.925 --> 00:01:19.035
What, what did you have in mind?

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.560
Well, let me give you mine and
then you'll respond appropriately.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:22.950
Okay.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.950
Um, I grew up middle class privileged
in the, in Chicago and my, and I

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.130
heard Yiddish all around, but nobody
expected me to understand any of it.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.730
And I didn't now I
can ... yiddish that comes later.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:36.180
Okay.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.860
That's another, that's another story.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:38.640
A very happy one.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.810
Um, uh, but I took French in high school.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:47.625
I got a passing grade in French from
my kind teacher under the condition

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:51.345
that I never take any more French
courses at that high school, cuz

00:01:51.345 --> 00:01:53.145
I was such a terrible student.

00:01:53.265 --> 00:01:53.655
All right.

00:01:53.955 --> 00:01:55.005
I had no interest in it.

00:01:55.009 --> 00:01:55.634
It was boring.

00:01:55.785 --> 00:01:56.985
I went to Hebrew school.

00:01:56.985 --> 00:01:58.365
It was beyond boredom.

00:01:58.905 --> 00:02:00.750
Then things happened.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.160
My mom set it up.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.130
So I would go on a tour, a bicycle tour,
a youth host tour of Europe, down the

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.840
Rhine Valley with a bunch of other kids.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:10.470
Really nice.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.960
We went to a youth host in Switzerland
and went to a party, nice kids everywhere.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:21.270
I sat around and listened to a
conversation, basically being, uh,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:26.010
managed by one young man who spoke
to some kids in French, some kids in

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.230
German and spoke to us in English.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.420
And I thought that is so cool.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.440
I wanna be like him when I grow
up, that really changed it.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.980
And I decided on the spot that
I would get good at languages.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.980
Uh, when the Peace Corps tour
was over, I went to Paris.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.490
The advice of one of our leaders of
the group, I went to the Alliance

00:02:45.609 --> 00:02:48.370
Française, took a couple of
weeks of French, got much better.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.830
Came back, took French, took German.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:55.820
And then the big breakthrough, my
major was music, piano and my piano

00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:00.050
teacher, Malcolm Bilson, who is
wonderful, um, suggested I go to

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.240
Vienna and study with his teacher.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:09.890
We had a terrible ... we had a horrible
argument, like one weekend and I, I

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:12.860
stopped with her, but I stayed in Vienna.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.500
My parents were so nice and so generous.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.150
Took German classes.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:19.920
Got really good in German.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.600
That was the real reason I got there.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:24.150
And this is the end of
this, uh, little segment.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.360
Um, everything I did was right.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.850
I had a group of friends in those days.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.280
English was not the lingua franca.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:35.070
You spoke, we spoke German and a
lot of them were better than I was.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.900
We recommended reading to each other,
which books were good and gradually

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.710
native speakers who liked to hang
out with foreigners joined the group.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:48.870
That was a wonderful, perfect
immersion experience that got me going.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.380
After that was the, oh, I'll just mention
three good things that happened in

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:56.310
Ethiopia that pushed me along: joined
the Peace Corps so I wouldn't have to

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.350
go to Vietnam and avoided the draft.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:03.915
Uh, I learned to speak Amharic which was
a lot of fun, cuz people were so friendly.

00:04:04.815 --> 00:04:06.674
Uh, and I met my wife there.

00:04:06.795 --> 00:04:11.025
She joined the Peace Corps cuz she was
an idealist and wanted to help people.

00:04:11.265 --> 00:04:14.924
So, and we're still married 55 years
later and it's still wonderful.

00:04:15.435 --> 00:04:20.055
So these are good things that
happened and I discovered coffee.

00:04:21.025 --> 00:04:21.315
Okay.

00:04:21.535 --> 00:04:22.055
Aha.

00:04:22.135 --> 00:04:22.595
Okay.

00:04:24.115 --> 00:04:24.455
Okay.

00:04:24.455 --> 00:04:25.755
Steve, tell us your story.

00:04:25.975 --> 00:04:30.105
Well, with specific reference to
learning languages, first of all, the

00:04:30.105 --> 00:04:33.435
number of things that you talked about
there, I can also confirm, first of

00:04:33.435 --> 00:04:39.255
all, uh, uh, you know, at school, I,
I, unlike you, I did get good grades

00:04:39.255 --> 00:04:41.795
in my French, uh, but I couldn't speak.

00:04:42.505 --> 00:04:46.530
And, uh, and that was typical, like in
Canada, in the French language, other

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.820
than French immersion, which is a recent
phenomenon in Canada, I dunno, 10,

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.020
20 years people in the regular French
stream don't learn to speak French.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.570
The better students get better grades.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.550
The poorer students get poorer grades.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.790
If one of the students has a
Francophone, Francophone parent,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.230
they're probably gonna do better.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.660
Uh, but by and large, they
don't learn in the classroom.

00:05:07.665 --> 00:05:09.855
What happened to me was I
went to McGill university.

00:05:09.855 --> 00:05:12.165
We had a, I took a course
in French civilization.

00:05:12.165 --> 00:05:17.505
We had a professor, Maurice Rabotan, who
was a phenomenal professor, not because

00:05:17.505 --> 00:05:22.575
he forced French at us, but because he
made the world of French civilization

00:05:22.575 --> 00:05:29.115
history so interesting to me that I got
totally motivated and living in Montreal,

00:05:29.145 --> 00:05:32.585
even though Montreal in those days was
two thirds French, one third English.

00:05:33.075 --> 00:05:35.385
And if you lived in the English
part of Montreal, you might

00:05:35.385 --> 00:05:36.864
just as well be in Chicago.

00:05:37.674 --> 00:05:41.114
Uh, but if you want to access the
French language, there's radio,

00:05:41.114 --> 00:05:44.984
there's newspapers, there's Montreal
University there's a whole bunch of

00:05:45.135 --> 00:05:49.285
things that you can do, including
watching, you know, movies.

00:05:49.285 --> 00:05:50.715
And, and eventually I went to France.

00:05:50.775 --> 00:05:57.750
So it, it was my motivation to sort of
my interest in the language and, uh, the

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:02.010
other thing too, uh, you mentioned that
you, uh, met your wife in a way related to

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:03.540
your interest in the world and so forth.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:06.480
And so did I, uh, I worked
for the Canadian government.

00:06:06.485 --> 00:06:10.260
I was sent to Hong Kong to learn Mandarin,
Chinese, and I met my wife in Hong Kong

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.110
and we've been married for 53 years.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:14.790
So we're catching up to you.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:21.539
Uh, but certainly I discovered then
that all of the instruction about the

00:06:21.539 --> 00:06:25.320
niceties of grammar and particularly
when the teachers select what you're

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.979
gonna read, so mostly you're not
interested in what they push at you.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:31.860
And in those days there wasn't
so much in the way of, you

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.360
know, there were no MP3 files.

00:06:33.599 --> 00:06:34.979
There weren't even cassette players.

00:06:34.979 --> 00:06:36.990
So there was very little listening.

00:06:36.995 --> 00:06:41.310
It was mostly reading with a
long list, word list and, and

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:42.480
things that were of no interest.

00:06:42.719 --> 00:06:45.599
Once you get actually motivated
in what you're reading about, it

00:06:45.599 --> 00:06:47.690
gets back to your compelling input.

00:06:48.075 --> 00:06:51.645
Then you don't notice you're being
carried along by your interest in the

00:06:51.645 --> 00:06:56.445
subject matter or your interest in
communicating with other people and

00:06:56.445 --> 00:06:58.245
the language just starts to pile in.

00:06:58.275 --> 00:07:01.455
So that, that has been my
experience, uh, with French.

00:07:03.224 --> 00:07:03.794
Oh boy.

00:07:04.245 --> 00:07:04.534
Yeah.

00:07:04.539 --> 00:07:05.815
So when did the change come?

00:07:06.765 --> 00:07:09.044
Well, like change be came very quickly.

00:07:09.044 --> 00:07:12.344
See, one of the things I think
is although I'm a critic of,

00:07:12.375 --> 00:07:14.775
um, school language instruction.

00:07:14.775 --> 00:07:19.110
In fact, if you spent 10
years passively doing stuff in

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.990
French despite the effort...

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:25.050
oh, the, the interesting thing
is I went to a, a football game.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:28.380
We have the Montreal Alouettes
and, uh, we had a problem with our

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:32.250
ticket and I had to explain this
to the usher who was a Francophone.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:37.590
And so I had to kind of find some French
words at age 16 or 17 and I still remember

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.930
to this day that all I had was words.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:40.970
I didn't have grammar.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:43.290
The teachers had only taught grammar.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:51.240
And all I could say was ... you
know,  like, I couldn't produce the

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.120
grammar, but what I did have was words.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:58.260
And so I'm, I remained to this
day convinced that the best sort

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.530
of way to evaluate your, at least
your potential in the language

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:03.030
is how many words do you know?

00:08:03.659 --> 00:08:05.730
You know, how many words
do you know, even passive.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.570
So, uh, but yeah, but eventually of
course, once I got really interested

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:12.450
and I watched movies and I listened
to radio and then I went to France

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:16.080
and I had to do all my schooling in
French and write essays in French

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:17.760
and answer oral questions in French.

00:08:17.765 --> 00:08:20.400
Of course then it just, it just cascades.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:22.050
Yeah.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:24.350
There you have it.

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:25.080
Very good.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:30.315
I have a question that one of our
members at LingQ put on our forum.

00:08:30.315 --> 00:08:33.015
And unfortunately Eric said he was
gonna find the name of this professor,

00:08:33.015 --> 00:08:36.825
but there was a professor who did
some work, which showed, purported to

00:08:36.825 --> 00:08:42.595
show that if you start speaking too
early, before you have 2000 hours of

00:08:42.615 --> 00:08:48.345
listening or some number, then you might
develop some bad pronunciation habits.

00:08:49.125 --> 00:08:53.610
Uh, Have you any thoughts on, you know,
when is the right time to start speaking?

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:57.120
That whole business and people I'm
sure they've asked you this before.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:02.640
How do we transition from acquiring the
language, getting the language, getting,

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.140
getting the brain used to the language.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:05.880
How do we get from there to output?

00:09:06.945 --> 00:09:07.215
Okay.

00:09:07.215 --> 00:09:09.315
I can give a partial answer to that.

00:09:09.315 --> 00:09:10.575
I don't know the complete answer.

00:09:11.115 --> 00:09:15.195
And that's a good research question
for eager scholars, I think, but

00:09:15.195 --> 00:09:18.375
there is a problem with early
speaking and I'll tell you my story.

00:09:19.125 --> 00:09:23.685
Um, we used to live down the street from
a family that had a daughter the same age

00:09:23.685 --> 00:09:27.675
as our daughter at the time around five
years old, which is nearly 50 years ago.

00:09:27.795 --> 00:09:28.065
Okay.

00:09:28.575 --> 00:09:32.165
And I used to get the, we'd
get the girls together to play.

00:09:32.645 --> 00:09:37.415
And one day it was my, my, um,
responsibility to pick up both girls after

00:09:37.415 --> 00:09:42.935
an hour, bring her to my house because
the mom had to go off and take her Spanish

00:09:42.935 --> 00:09:45.995
course at the local community college.

00:09:46.475 --> 00:09:48.334
So I went up, I said,
okay, girls, let's go.

00:09:48.574 --> 00:09:50.454
She said, wait, I have to take my pill.

00:09:51.295 --> 00:09:53.340
She goes in the bathroom takes this pill.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:54.810
And again, we are good family friends.

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:56.100
I said, what was that?

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:02.550
She said, well, today Valium, today
would be Prozac, anti-anxiety medication.

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:07.050
So again, relying on our close friendship,
I said, well, why are you taking Valium?

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:09.760
French class, it freaks me out.

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:13.740
And I said, what is it about French
class that makes you so nervous?

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:19.620
When the teacher calls on me
having to talk before I'm ready.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:23.250
I interpret that as having to use
stuff you've consciously learned

00:10:23.579 --> 00:10:25.800
and not being able to work on...

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.329
to use what you have acquired.

00:10:27.689 --> 00:10:28.800
I thought this is something.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:31.859
I looked at all the research
found more and it's all there.

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:35.640
One of the biggest anxiety producing
things is having to talk before

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:36.959
you're ready in a language class.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.900
Now there's some interesting
anthropological research

00:10:39.959 --> 00:10:41.010
that backs this up.

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:45.474
A guy named Sorenson went to the
Amazon Valley and looked at a group

00:10:46.525 --> 00:10:51.505
of 10,000 people on the border
between two countries who spoke

00:10:51.954 --> 00:10:55.675
like 24 languages, not all related.

00:10:55.885 --> 00:10:57.474
10,000 is not that much.

00:10:57.834 --> 00:11:00.685
You go to a football game there're
10,000 people in the stadium.

00:11:00.685 --> 00:11:04.074
That's not very much, but, and so
this is really not a big number.

00:11:05.459 --> 00:11:07.079
They were all pretty good in languages.

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:10.530
They picked up mommy's language,
daddy's language, language of their

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:13.860
friends, gradually the language
of all the local lingua franca

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:15.209
and were heavily monolingual.

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.219
So Sorenson interviewed them.

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:18.209
How do you do it?

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:20.610
We don't try to say anything.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:24.000
We don't force it unless
we think we're ready.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.699
We just wait, we just listen.

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:29.140
And then it comes.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:32.640
By the way in that culture, you
are forbidden from correcting

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:34.349
anybody for doing anything.

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:34.860
Okay.

00:11:35.099 --> 00:11:36.540
Other studies have found the same thing.

00:11:36.719 --> 00:11:40.410
We go to another country and we
just stay there, be with the people

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:43.860
until we can hear the language,
which means understand it.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:48.270
They say for them consistent
results, it takes a year or

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:49.689
two before you're comfortable.

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:53.640
We can actually, I think that's
true in the real environment where

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:54.900
you don't have extra support.

00:11:55.905 --> 00:11:56.595
We can do better.

00:11:56.985 --> 00:11:58.125
We have language classes.

00:11:58.815 --> 00:12:02.805
We can give people comprehensible input
right away in a good language class,

00:12:02.805 --> 00:12:04.035
which is what we're supposed to do.

00:12:04.455 --> 00:12:08.055
So that backs up the point, the
reluctance with early speaking,

00:12:08.385 --> 00:12:09.825
it could give you a bad accent.

00:12:09.825 --> 00:12:15.375
I don't know, let's find out, but
it's the fear, the, uh, discomfort of

00:12:15.375 --> 00:12:16.965
having to produce before you're ready.

00:12:18.375 --> 00:12:23.940
Uh, and, and I would add in my experience,
I am more say intimidated, say I

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.310
have an online lesson with a tutor.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:31.260
It bothers me more when I don't understand
what the other person is saying.

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:36.240
Uh, the fact that I stumble, especially
if it's with a tutor, so you're in a

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:38.849
protected environment, that's normal.

00:12:38.849 --> 00:12:41.099
It's, it's, it's mildly annoying.

00:12:41.099 --> 00:12:44.489
That words that I have been reading
and listening to, and I'm sure I know

00:12:44.489 --> 00:12:48.390
the meaning of, and I've gone over it
many times and I get in front of my

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:50.130
tutor and I cannot find those words.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.140
Those words are just not available to me.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:56.640
But if I understand what the
teacher tutor is saying, I'm happy.

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:00.900
If on the other hand, I don't
understand that's a very uncomfortable.

00:13:01.425 --> 00:13:07.035
Um, so yeah, I'm, I'm a firm believer
in that, but again, so, so, so then to

00:13:07.035 --> 00:13:10.455
me, the question is like, how do you
evaluate someone in the language in a

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:12.105
way we shouldn't be evaluating others.

00:13:12.105 --> 00:13:14.115
We shouldn't even be evaluating ourselves.

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:19.795
We should just enjoy the process, but
people do wanna know, well, am I B1 or B2?

00:13:19.815 --> 00:13:24.675
You know, am I gonna take, take
the HSK or whatever TOEFL test?

00:13:25.245 --> 00:13:31.380
Uh, so do you measure it entirely on
how well a person speaks or how well

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:35.220
they pronounce or, or, uh, how well
they understand and how do you even

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:36.990
measure their level of comprehension?

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:38.760
This it's, it's a big issue.

00:13:38.765 --> 00:13:39.900
Like how do you evaluate people?

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:45.255
I have never been able to stay awake
during a discussion of testing and

00:13:45.255 --> 00:13:48.555
how we should do it, cuz just for
the reasons you mentioned, it's an

00:13:48.555 --> 00:13:52.965
external, uh, task imposed on us
and that's not what we're about.

00:13:53.565 --> 00:13:53.775
Right?

00:13:53.775 --> 00:13:56.325
Uh, just the tests pretty
well agree with each other.

00:13:56.355 --> 00:13:57.550
So that's not a problem.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:01.635
So it's, it's a question I
really would love to ignore.

00:14:01.695 --> 00:14:03.135
Can we go onto the next one?

00:14:03.135 --> 00:14:03.525
All right.

00:14:03.525 --> 00:14:04.485
No, I agree with you.

00:14:04.995 --> 00:14:05.565
I agree with that.

00:14:05.565 --> 00:14:08.535
I'll give you, I'll give you
some results from testing, but

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:10.145
I don't the best way of testing.

00:14:11.135 --> 00:14:12.545
Maybe self evaluation.

00:14:13.155 --> 00:14:16.365
I also agree with you about,
uh, correction and you

00:14:16.365 --> 00:14:17.385
often hear the argument,

00:14:17.415 --> 00:14:20.985
well, you know, mothers correct
their kids when they start speaking,

00:14:20.985 --> 00:14:22.515
otherwise they'd never speak correctly.

00:14:23.235 --> 00:14:23.785
That's an...

00:14:24.285 --> 00:14:26.025
what's your reaction to that comment?

00:14:26.505 --> 00:14:27.435
Actually, they don't.

00:14:29.025 --> 00:14:29.645
Of course they don't.

00:14:30.155 --> 00:14:30.725
Yeah, of course.

00:14:31.334 --> 00:14:33.944
They couldn't possibly
correct every little mistake.

00:14:34.245 --> 00:14:38.625
And I've noticed this myself when
I do give lessons in English, if

00:14:38.625 --> 00:14:41.295
I correct the other person, they
continue making the same mistake

00:14:41.295 --> 00:14:42.855
right there in the very same lesson.

00:14:43.185 --> 00:14:44.415
So there's no impact.

00:14:44.865 --> 00:14:49.064
And, uh, I find it mildly
annoying if I am corrected without

00:14:49.069 --> 00:14:50.444
having asked for corrections.

00:14:50.444 --> 00:14:52.814
So to me, the whole correction
issue is I agree that's one of

00:14:52.814 --> 00:14:56.324
your themes is that it just creates
more, more tension, more Prozac.

00:14:57.015 --> 00:14:59.955
Also it is aimed at conscious learning.

00:15:00.985 --> 00:15:01.705
Make a mistake.

00:15:01.725 --> 00:15:05.505
The child says I comes to school
every day and you say, no, no.

00:15:05.505 --> 00:15:06.855
It's I come to school every day.

00:15:07.185 --> 00:15:10.395
The child is supposed to think, oh
yeah, that S goes on the third person

00:15:10.395 --> 00:15:11.865
singular, not the first person...

00:15:12.084 --> 00:15:13.515
that you look at the studies.

00:15:13.694 --> 00:15:14.265
It does...

00:15:14.265 --> 00:15:16.005
it is a big disappointment.

00:15:16.005 --> 00:15:19.064
As a matter of fact, testing
doesn't really work that well.

00:15:20.535 --> 00:15:22.815
So what, uh, what has changed?

00:15:22.815 --> 00:15:28.275
I mean, I mentioned in my video that
I first met you in 2003, by the way, I

00:15:28.275 --> 00:15:32.795
didn't know when it was that I first met
you, but then I managed to find the Taiwan

00:15:32.985 --> 00:15:39.255
lectures book that I bought in Taipei,
and it said they're published in 2003.

00:15:39.405 --> 00:15:42.765
And it just so happened that my book
about my own experiences of language

00:15:42.765 --> 00:15:45.015
learning was also published in 2003.

00:15:45.435 --> 00:15:46.605
And I went to Taipei.

00:15:46.605 --> 00:15:49.275
I was at this language conference,
the first one I'd ever attended.

00:15:49.765 --> 00:15:53.605
And everybody was thronging into
this lecture room to listen to Dr.

00:15:53.605 --> 00:15:58.135
Krashen and I didn't know who Dr
Krashen was, but I remember that I

00:15:58.135 --> 00:16:01.585
was so impressed with the sort of
element of simplicity that you brought

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:04.315
to the process of language learning
that I quickly had to buy your book.

00:16:04.825 --> 00:16:07.955
What has changed in language
learning since 2003?

00:16:09.405 --> 00:16:11.025
Not much.

00:16:12.105 --> 00:16:13.725
And let me tell you why.

00:16:13.785 --> 00:16:14.685
Good question.

00:16:14.775 --> 00:16:18.015
Good question means when I'm prepared
to answer, and this is one of them

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:21.855
that I enjoy answer, uh, here and
there yes, there have been changes.

00:16:22.035 --> 00:16:25.694
There's more comprehensible input
than before, but by and large language

00:16:25.699 --> 00:16:28.555
teaching is the same as it was in 1945.

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:28.814
Okay.

00:16:29.085 --> 00:16:31.215
There's been very little change
and there are good reasons.

00:16:31.215 --> 00:16:33.035
Why, how do you find out about it?

00:16:33.635 --> 00:16:35.450
Well, you can buy books.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:39.650
They're, uh, written in, except
for mine they're generally written

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:41.540
what we call tortured prose.

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:44.360
Uh, you don't understand what
the person's talking about.

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:45.350
I can't get through them.

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:48.530
Most of the time professional
journals, the same way.

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:51.230
They're also expensive.

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:55.760
Um, my books, little commercial
message, my books are all for free.

00:16:56.370 --> 00:17:00.120
I've put, if you follow me on, uh,
Twitter, you find where to find them.

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:03.750
I, you can download them for free cuz
otherwise nobody's gonna read them.

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:04.889
I can't afford them.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:06.510
Nobody else can afford them.

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:13.349
The solution to all this is what is
called open access, getting our stuff

00:17:13.355 --> 00:17:16.200
out so real people can read it and do it.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:20.069
Otherwise, nothing is going to
happen cuz nobody can afford this.

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:22.349
That's why we came very little change.

00:17:22.379 --> 00:17:27.895
Oh, some positive change came for
me a couple of years ago when the

00:17:27.895 --> 00:17:33.505
pandemic started and I offered to
give free webinars free seminars over

00:17:33.505 --> 00:17:36.235
the next year, I did like 70 of them.

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:39.720
That helped no question.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:40.800
I can't do that anymore.

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:41.580
Cause I can't afford it.

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:43.710
Now I charge a little bit, not a lot.

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:44.129
Okay.

00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:45.360
That was a commercial message.

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:48.840
Um, but it's hearing about it under...

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:50.850
once they hear it and
understand it, it works.

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:53.520
Getting the word out has been slow.

00:17:54.115 --> 00:17:58.225
And I don't know any magic way, cuz people
are still writing incomprehensible papers.

00:17:59.245 --> 00:18:03.835
But you know, obviously the way we
learn languages, the way our brains

00:18:03.835 --> 00:18:05.305
learn languages, hasn't changed.

00:18:05.305 --> 00:18:08.335
That has to be the same, whether
you're in the Amazon or wherever you

00:18:08.335 --> 00:18:16.225
are, uh, in so far as the awareness of
comprehensible input, uh, in the language

00:18:16.225 --> 00:18:20.305
class, in the schools, my impression
is that there's increasing awareness.

00:18:20.485 --> 00:18:22.195
The few times that I've attended the.

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:23.280
What is it?

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:29.149
ACE, American association of teachers,
of whatever it is, AFT something.

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:30.570
Yeah.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:34.919
You and other proponents of
comprehensible input are extremely

00:18:34.919 --> 00:18:36.780
popular amongst the teachers.

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:43.350
Amongst some teachers, the ones who've
kept up with the research, and it's hard

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:47.550
for teachers to keep up, cuz they're
overwhelmed with work and the stuff

00:18:47.550 --> 00:18:49.139
is incomprehensible and expensive.

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:49.950
Right.

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:52.330
There have been some
conversions and that'schelped.

00:18:52.815 --> 00:18:54.715
The big one of course is Steve Kaufmann.

00:18:54.765 --> 00:18:55.185
Okay.

00:18:55.785 --> 00:18:57.795
People have begun to
understand the research.

00:18:58.125 --> 00:19:01.125
Bill van Patton has been
extremely supportive.

00:19:01.395 --> 00:19:05.655
Earl Stevick in the old days, that's
helped a lot, but it still is not

00:19:05.685 --> 00:19:07.665
part of common, common knowledge.

00:19:08.565 --> 00:19:10.845
But, but I think another
thing that has changed.

00:19:11.025 --> 00:19:16.275
So if I go back say 50 years or whatever,
55 years ago, when I was learning Mandarin

00:19:16.275 --> 00:19:19.005
Chinese, we had an open reel tape.

00:19:20.445 --> 00:19:23.535
And there was not very much
audio material available.

00:19:24.135 --> 00:19:28.305
And, um, there were books that were
this thick that you could buy with,

00:19:28.365 --> 00:19:31.275
with, uh, things that you could
read with, with vocabulary list.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:34.425
And I remember even 20, 30
years ago, I was approached

00:19:34.425 --> 00:19:36.285
by a local community college.

00:19:36.285 --> 00:19:40.185
Would I, you know, contribute towards
a language lab and the budget for

00:19:40.185 --> 00:19:42.255
the language lab is like $50,000.

00:19:42.885 --> 00:19:51.480
And today I carry on my iPhone a quantity
of audio and text material, obviously,

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:55.640
with, with LingQ, of course, with
access to online dictionaries, with a

00:19:55.645 --> 00:20:00.780
tremendous range of functions and the
ability to access individuals who are

00:20:00.785 --> 00:20:05.640
offering, you know, content comprehensible
input in French, in, in Chinese.

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:11.100
And all of that stuff now is available on
my iPhone far more valuable than anything

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:13.710
that was in that $50,000 language lab.

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:16.605
So surely a lot has changed.

00:20:17.985 --> 00:20:18.165
Yeah.

00:20:18.165 --> 00:20:18.764
We'll see.

00:20:18.825 --> 00:20:20.385
I haven't seen it yet, but we'll see.

00:20:20.415 --> 00:20:21.825
We'll hope that this gets around.

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:22.544
Absolutely.

00:20:22.935 --> 00:20:25.274
So you're saying teachers are
not taking advantage of this?

00:20:25.665 --> 00:20:27.465
Now it's the knowledge is not yet there.

00:20:27.465 --> 00:20:28.215
We're getting better.

00:20:28.784 --> 00:20:31.754
Now that you mention ACTFL let me
introduce a little bit of gossip.

00:20:32.835 --> 00:20:34.754
You and I were, and I will embarrass you.

00:20:34.754 --> 00:20:36.675
You and I were in an actual meeting once.

00:20:37.004 --> 00:20:42.975
And I was there and I met my former,
uh, Chinese teacher, uh, ... and we,

00:20:43.215 --> 00:20:47.804
and, uh, she invited you to come to
lunch with all the Chinese teachers.

00:20:48.854 --> 00:20:51.284
I'll give you the word, they
thought you were fantastic.

00:20:51.705 --> 00:20:54.284
Oh, they thought
your ... was just excellent.

00:20:54.284 --> 00:20:55.725
And they loved what you said.

00:20:56.024 --> 00:20:57.314
So live with that.

00:20:57.344 --> 00:20:57.705
Okay.

00:20:57.975 --> 00:20:58.334
Okay.

00:20:58.485 --> 00:20:59.534
I'll try to live with it.

00:20:59.774 --> 00:21:00.104
Thank you.

00:21:01.935 --> 00:21:07.215
But, uh, so, so the world, I think
the, the, uh, One of the things that

00:21:07.215 --> 00:21:10.740
I find annoying say here in say in
British Columbia, which is not New

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:16.005
York or Chicago, schools will not offer
certain languages because they say we

00:21:16.005 --> 00:21:20.115
don't have a qualified teacher or a
certified teacher for that language.

00:21:20.325 --> 00:21:24.525
However, I've always felt if you
had a person who was knowledgeable

00:21:24.525 --> 00:21:28.695
about how to use the resources on the
internet, the teacher doesn't have

00:21:28.695 --> 00:21:33.165
to be the one providing the language,
doesn't have to be the one that we

00:21:33.165 --> 00:21:35.054
learn from explaining the language.

00:21:35.325 --> 00:21:41.355
You just need a, a guide, a coach, someone
who can help so that if student A wants

00:21:41.355 --> 00:21:46.185
to learn Spanish and student B wants to
learn Chinese or Tagalog or whatever,

00:21:46.304 --> 00:21:47.745
there are enough resources out there.

00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:51.764
Why is it that schools are hung
up on having a certified certified

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:54.150
teacher before they will offer
that language at the school?

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:59.445
You have just recruited me to your
team, that's a wonderful thing.

00:21:59.445 --> 00:22:03.915
I haven't thought about it because I'm
not an iPhone person other than just

00:22:03.915 --> 00:22:05.805
basic needs and not into all this stuff.

00:22:06.345 --> 00:22:08.595
And it's all around me and I
have pretty much ignored it.

00:22:08.685 --> 00:22:12.585
I, you just convinced me,
this is a wonderful way of

00:22:12.705 --> 00:22:14.295
solving the problem I think.

00:22:14.805 --> 00:22:18.045
Yeah, there are so many
resources out there now.

00:22:18.675 --> 00:22:22.455
And, and again, if I look at Canada
where, you know, you must learn French.

00:22:22.455 --> 00:22:24.045
So here we are in British Columbia.

00:22:24.405 --> 00:22:27.915
We're far away from French
speakers god bless them on

00:22:27.915 --> 00:22:29.265
the other side of the country.

00:22:29.655 --> 00:22:34.095
So you're expecting kids to learn
French out of some kind of civic duty

00:22:34.305 --> 00:22:35.955
because it's an official language.

00:22:36.225 --> 00:22:37.935
That's not very motivating.

00:22:38.265 --> 00:22:43.530
What if the kid, children, boys,
girls wanna learn Spanish or Swahili.

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:47.280
Why wouldn't you give them a
victory in learning the language

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:48.720
that they're motivated to learn?

00:22:49.169 --> 00:22:53.070
Once they've got one language under
their belt,they will be in a better

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:56.399
position to learn French if at some
point they become motivated to do so.

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:59.970
And if they don't become motivated
to learn French, they will not learn.

00:23:01.995 --> 00:23:03.855
Absolutely correct.

00:23:04.215 --> 00:23:05.085
I think you're right.

00:23:05.625 --> 00:23:07.215
I'm I'm part of your team now.

00:23:07.485 --> 00:23:10.395
Can I tell you my only French
joke that I managed to pull off?

00:23:10.495 --> 00:23:11.655
Is it, is it clean?

00:23:12.295 --> 00:23:12.975
More than.

00:23:13.305 --> 00:23:20.175
I was in, um, in, uh, Montreal with
a group of Anglophones and we were in

00:23:20.175 --> 00:23:24.705
a French area and we went to dinner
and this is my greatest triumph.

00:23:24.705 --> 00:23:25.005
Okay.

00:23:25.005 --> 00:23:25.845
I'm really proud of this.

00:23:26.415 --> 00:23:31.185
The waiter came over and said ... I
raised my hand and I said

00:23:34.305 --> 00:23:36.015
...
the worst mistake you can ever make.

00:23:36.855 --> 00:23:37.965
He understood what you were saying.

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:38.955
Of course he laughed.

00:23:38.955 --> 00:23:39.615
He thought it was great.

00:23:40.125 --> 00:23:40.545
Okay.

00:23:42.195 --> 00:23:42.705
That was it.

00:23:42.915 --> 00:23:43.695
That's the story?

00:23:44.055 --> 00:23:44.635
That's it.

00:23:46.085 --> 00:23:46.465
Why...

00:23:46.995 --> 00:23:50.004
but you know, that's a good example.

00:23:50.325 --> 00:23:51.445
So what?

00:23:52.025 --> 00:23:53.500
He understood what you were saying.

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:55.060
You mean because you didn't say...

00:23:55.220 --> 00:23:57.480
I did it on purpose.

00:23:57.480 --> 00:23:58.110
Let me tell you.

00:23:58.170 --> 00:23:58.470
Okay.

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:00.470
It was a joke and he
liked it and he laughed.

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:01.200
Okay.

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:05.450
But, but the point is, even if you
didn't do it on purpose, so what?

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:09.370
And I get, like, if I do a video
in some other language and I make a

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:12.750
mistake, then there's always some people
who jump on me and stuff like that.

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:15.480
I mean like why, why does it matter?

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:20.460
Uh, I have never spoken to anyone in
a foreign language, say in, in English

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:25.450
where I felt, you know, I respected that
person less because they made a mistake.

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:30.735
This is a big, and this is again, I'm
sorry to say all these nice things

00:24:30.735 --> 00:24:31.995
about you, but I have no choice.

00:24:32.564 --> 00:24:37.965
Um, when I had just read what, in
one of your, uh, papers, you said,

00:24:37.965 --> 00:24:39.254
don't worry about making mistakes.

00:24:39.675 --> 00:24:40.760
Nobody cares.

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:42.885
I'm more interested in what you say.

00:24:43.665 --> 00:24:47.475
I was in Mexico at a,
at a one week session.

00:24:49.365 --> 00:24:54.794
And I had lunch and dinner with, uh,
Spanish teachersand we spoke Spanish

00:24:54.794 --> 00:24:58.485
the whole time and I thought, and I
thought, gee, I'm gonna make mistakes.

00:24:58.695 --> 00:25:02.264
And then I had just read
what you wrote and it hit.

00:25:02.835 --> 00:25:07.235
I had the best time ever,
very little ..., mostly

00:25:07.754 --> 00:25:09.014
...
we gossiped the whole time.

00:25:10.185 --> 00:25:12.555
Who was it who said, if you don't
have anything good to say about

00:25:12.555 --> 00:25:13.935
someone, come sit next to me?

00:25:14.425 --> 00:25:14.735
Okay.

00:25:15.095 --> 00:25:16.725
It was so much fun.

00:25:16.815 --> 00:25:19.125
Thanks to you not worrying about mistakes.

00:25:20.085 --> 00:25:23.415
And it gets back to your point
about the Prozac pill or whatever.

00:25:23.805 --> 00:25:25.365
And you've said this there's...

00:25:25.585 --> 00:25:28.935
unfortunately, I mean, people should go
to your website and read your material,

00:25:29.025 --> 00:25:33.885
but there's so many quotable quotes,
but this, this, what do you call it?

00:25:33.885 --> 00:25:34.585
The emo....

00:25:35.939 --> 00:25:37.439
emotive filter, no.

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:39.120
The affective filter

00:25:39.430 --> 00:25:40.409
affective filter.

00:25:41.129 --> 00:25:48.090
Uh, there's a formula I think, attributed
to Lomb Kato that sort of has, you know,

00:25:48.179 --> 00:25:55.439
your attitude plus the time you spend with
the language and divided by inhibition,

00:25:55.439 --> 00:25:57.030
frustration, all this negative stuff.

00:25:58.110 --> 00:26:01.890
Obviously you wanna have a positive
attitude, you wanna spend the time, but

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:06.420
if you can reduce the inhibition, the
fear you're only gonna learn faster.

