00:39:11.970 --> 00:39:12.839
listening to understand.

00:39:13.234 --> 00:39:16.234
Uh, but other than that, and the other
thing about listening, of course, it's

00:39:16.234 --> 00:39:18.065
easier to do so I can do it in my car.

00:39:18.065 --> 00:39:20.585
I can do it while making
breakfast, reading.

00:39:20.615 --> 00:39:21.365
I'm committed.

00:39:21.370 --> 00:39:24.214
I gotta sit there and read, I can't
read and drive my car at the same time.

00:39:24.634 --> 00:39:27.785
So I think it's a function
of what's available to you.

00:39:28.325 --> 00:39:32.254
Uh, what time you have to
devote to one or the other task.

00:39:32.285 --> 00:39:34.924
Uh, I, and also what you like to do.

00:39:34.955 --> 00:39:38.585
So I'm, I think I'm always reluctant
is that you should be 60% reading

00:39:38.585 --> 00:39:40.055
and 40% listening or something.

00:39:40.055 --> 00:39:41.355
I think depends on the individual.

00:39:42.390 --> 00:39:43.904
Yeah, no answer to that.

00:39:44.805 --> 00:39:47.685
Here's another one of these questions
that I don't think there's an answer to.

00:39:47.895 --> 00:39:50.805
When is the right time to come
back to a previous language?

00:39:51.765 --> 00:39:54.065
I'd say, uh, seven o'clock in the evening.

00:39:59.605 --> 00:40:02.895
You know, again, it's, it's so
dependent on your circumstance.

00:40:02.895 --> 00:40:08.175
If you're about to go to Mexico and you
haven't looked at Spanish for six years,

00:40:08.355 --> 00:40:11.685
that's the time to start refreshing
your me, your Spanish, you know?

00:40:12.915 --> 00:40:16.275
Uh, what do you think about
the practice of reading aloud?

00:40:18.105 --> 00:40:19.995
Uh, listening to someone else read aloud.

00:40:20.025 --> 00:40:21.195
Fantastic.

00:40:21.525 --> 00:40:25.365
I don't know that your reading aloud
does any good at all it's output and

00:40:25.365 --> 00:40:26.595
you're not paying attention to meaning.

00:40:27.315 --> 00:40:28.095
I agree.

00:40:28.095 --> 00:40:30.705
And again, it's one of these
things that I have tried and have

00:40:30.705 --> 00:40:32.835
never been able to continue doing.

00:40:33.555 --> 00:40:33.735
Right.

00:40:34.635 --> 00:40:37.425
I would, it feels boring
before I even start.

00:40:37.875 --> 00:40:38.265
Right.

00:40:38.775 --> 00:40:40.665
Here's one though that
comes up all the time.

00:40:41.025 --> 00:40:45.085
What do you consider fluent
to be fluent in a language?

00:40:46.965 --> 00:40:47.525
Wow.

00:40:48.345 --> 00:40:53.505
I, it depends so much on
circumstance, on situations.

00:40:53.595 --> 00:40:54.045
Okay.

00:40:54.735 --> 00:41:00.405
In some situations I'm actually fluent
and very impressive in languages I can

00:41:00.410 --> 00:41:03.795
hardly speak and depends on the person.

00:41:04.095 --> 00:41:05.415
Uh, that's so true.

00:41:05.625 --> 00:41:07.485
So it is incredibly variable.

00:41:07.935 --> 00:41:10.155
Again, I invite someone
else to start working on it.

00:41:11.595 --> 00:41:13.065
I, I, my own view.

00:41:14.040 --> 00:41:18.899
If you can use the language in a way
that you are not uncomfortable and

00:41:18.899 --> 00:41:22.919
the person listening to you is not
uncomfortable, then you're fluent.

00:41:23.640 --> 00:41:27.750
And I, if we wanted to be more
specific, I tend to see it

00:41:27.750 --> 00:41:30.000
as B2 on the European scale.

00:41:30.060 --> 00:41:34.230
In other words, you have enough vocabulary
that you're likely to understand much

00:41:34.230 --> 00:41:36.029
of what a native speaker says to you.

00:41:36.419 --> 00:41:40.200
And you're likely to be able to express
yourself on a wide range of subjects.

00:41:40.259 --> 00:41:41.310
Even with mistakes.

00:41:41.370 --> 00:41:42.180
To me, that's fluent.

00:41:43.090 --> 00:41:45.180
God, you just made me fluent in Spanish.

00:41:45.180 --> 00:41:45.600
Thank you.

00:41:46.170 --> 00:41:46.680
All right.

00:41:46.890 --> 00:41:47.370
Fantastic.

00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:51.660
And here's another one: can
full phonological acquisition

00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:53.490
be achieved through input alone?

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:55.080
Good question one.

00:41:55.085 --> 00:41:56.040
I'm prepared to answer.

00:41:56.310 --> 00:41:56.760
Okay.

00:41:56.760 --> 00:41:57.300
Same thing.

00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:02.030
Um, I think we're a lot better in
pronunciation than we think we are.

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:07.140
Uh, in fact, I think the ability
to perfectly acquire accents is

00:42:07.140 --> 00:42:11.640
there the language acquisition
device never shuts off.

00:42:12.120 --> 00:42:16.170
We don't use our best
accents cause we feel silly.

00:42:16.740 --> 00:42:19.650
I wanna relate to you the
in incident that started it.

00:42:19.960 --> 00:42:23.670
It happened when I was teaching in
Ethiopia a long time ago, and there was

00:42:23.675 --> 00:42:27.300
a, a British guy visiting our school,
worked for the British council, I think.

00:42:27.570 --> 00:42:32.670
And we talked about his experiences in
high school, French, uh, Secondary school.

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:35.580
He said the final exam you had
to get up in front of your French

00:42:35.580 --> 00:42:36.990
teachers and speak to them.

00:42:37.230 --> 00:42:41.850
Nothing is more humiliating for a
young man than they have to do that.

00:42:42.300 --> 00:42:46.080
So he decided to come in and
make fun of the teachers and show

00:42:46.080 --> 00:42:47.550
them up for the fools they were.

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:49.260
He dressed French.

00:42:49.620 --> 00:42:50.400
He had a beret.

00:42:51.105 --> 00:42:54.675
He had a glass of what looked
like wine and he started to speak.

00:42:54.675 --> 00:42:55.395
He says, ah

00:43:01.015 --> 00:43:02.705
...
he thought he was making fun of them.

00:43:03.665 --> 00:43:05.535
He got the highest grades possible.

00:43:05.984 --> 00:43:07.845
He said, where have you been?

00:43:08.115 --> 00:43:08.924
That's great.

00:43:09.225 --> 00:43:11.295
Why haven't you ever done that in class?

00:43:11.924 --> 00:43:16.005
We can't do that because we're
not a member of the group.

00:43:16.035 --> 00:43:16.904
It's not us.

00:43:17.234 --> 00:43:20.475
Peter Ustinov in an interview,
said - his French is very good.

00:43:20.475 --> 00:43:21.345
I've heard him in movies.

00:43:21.345 --> 00:43:25.125
He sounds absolutely perfect,
but he doesn't use that accent

00:43:25.154 --> 00:43:28.785
when he speaks to native speakers
of French, he has an accent.

00:43:29.025 --> 00:43:34.795
We have an output filter and it's
because accent is a marker of group

00:43:35.095 --> 00:43:37.795
membership of club membership.

00:43:38.245 --> 00:43:41.545
Uh, when I made someone from,
uh, London, I don't start

00:43:41.545 --> 00:43:43.075
speaking with a British accent.

00:43:44.275 --> 00:43:45.535
I can a little bit.

00:43:45.625 --> 00:43:46.135
Okay.

00:43:46.555 --> 00:43:49.795
But it seems funny, awkward,
and maybe even feels insulting.

00:43:50.965 --> 00:43:53.475
So we have these powerful,
effective blocks.

00:43:53.805 --> 00:43:56.065
We're a lot better than we think we are.

00:43:57.085 --> 00:43:58.315
I agree with you.

00:43:59.190 --> 00:44:03.660
I have said in the past that in order to
pronounce better, even to learn languages

00:44:03.660 --> 00:44:09.120
better, we have to have a sort of an
attitude of cultural weightlessness,

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:14.790
you know, and, and, you know, I lived
in Japan and or if a, if say a north

00:44:14.790 --> 00:44:19.170
American Anglo Saxon English speaker is
very proud of being, you know, English

00:44:19.170 --> 00:44:22.215
speaker they're reluctant to be foolish.

00:44:22.634 --> 00:44:26.925
They're reluctant to just be
culturally weightless and just try

00:44:26.925 --> 00:44:31.035
to hear the language and repeat
the language and just go for it.

00:44:31.040 --> 00:44:32.445
So I totally agree with
what you're saying.

00:44:32.535 --> 00:44:33.465
That's been my experience.

00:44:34.485 --> 00:44:34.725
Yeah.

00:44:35.025 --> 00:44:37.965
I, but it is hard for the
public to accept this.

00:44:39.165 --> 00:44:43.634
I got a, um, invitation to meet with
someone I was told was extremely

00:44:43.634 --> 00:44:46.964
wealthy business person from
Germany, living in Los Angeles.

00:44:47.355 --> 00:44:52.365
And I was driven to his, uh,
house up in the Hills, expensive.

00:44:52.605 --> 00:44:53.895
He wanted to get rid of his accent.

00:44:55.260 --> 00:44:57.360
And I gave him my little theory there.

00:44:57.840 --> 00:45:00.000
Uh, and he said, no, that's ridiculous.

00:45:00.030 --> 00:45:03.660
I said, what if I got you a sound
spectrograph and we got native

00:45:03.660 --> 00:45:06.780
speakers of English and you
try to max match their pattern.

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:07.740
Yes.

00:45:08.040 --> 00:45:08.970
That's how to do it.

00:45:09.330 --> 00:45:10.470
I said, it's never gonna work.

00:45:10.560 --> 00:45:13.050
He showed me the door
immediately and told me to leave.

00:45:14.160 --> 00:45:16.380
People would never believed
this it's there within us.

00:45:16.380 --> 00:45:17.070
We can do it.

00:45:17.490 --> 00:45:18.300
Don't worry about it.

00:45:18.330 --> 00:45:19.865
There's good reason we have an accent.

00:45:20.585 --> 00:45:26.580
See, A I personally, if I hear
someone who has, uh, a noticeable

00:45:26.580 --> 00:45:28.770
foreign accent, but uses the language.

00:45:28.770 --> 00:45:31.440
Well, I, I respect those people.

00:45:31.440 --> 00:45:35.190
In fact, more than someone
who has almost no accent.

00:45:35.399 --> 00:45:39.330
So there there's no advantage in if
you're being, if people understand you,

00:45:39.509 --> 00:45:43.440
there's no real advantage in being like
mistaken for a native, which I don't

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:45.690
think is achievable for most people.

00:45:46.379 --> 00:45:50.069
Uh, also I think getting back
to what you were saying, we have

00:45:50.075 --> 00:45:51.330
to wanna be part of that group.

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:54.060
So every time I've, you know,
tried to learn the language,

00:45:54.390 --> 00:45:56.040
Japanese, French, Chinese, whatever.

00:45:56.190 --> 00:45:57.240
I wanna be one of them.

00:45:57.270 --> 00:46:01.200
I don't see myself as this foreigner
trying to learn this language.

00:46:01.589 --> 00:46:04.355
If I'm in a group with
them, I'm one of them.

00:46:04.385 --> 00:46:07.475
That's that kind of
attitude I think helps us.

00:46:07.865 --> 00:46:10.955
And so I do believe that that, and oh,
and the other thing I was gonna say.

00:46:12.630 --> 00:46:12.900
Okay.

00:46:12.900 --> 00:46:16.260
An actor, an actress, they
have to play another role.

00:46:16.290 --> 00:46:16.620
Right?

00:46:16.950 --> 00:46:21.840
So they are very good at accents because
they're used to the idea of seeing

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:27.630
themselves as not John Smith, but as
this character that they're playing.

00:46:27.870 --> 00:46:30.600
And, and on one last note, there
was this American actress whose name

00:46:30.600 --> 00:46:33.000
escapes me, but she has a phenomen.

00:46:33.625 --> 00:46:36.565
Her pronunciation in French is
flawless, but she still makes

00:46:36.565 --> 00:46:38.095
tremendous mistakes with gender.

00:46:38.335 --> 00:46:38.725
Yeah.

00:46:39.805 --> 00:46:40.794
But like, it's amazing.

00:46:40.794 --> 00:46:44.305
She speaks like a French woman, but
the gender is just so difficult to

00:46:44.305 --> 00:46:48.685
get the hang of cuz we don't have it
in English, but she, as an actress,

00:46:48.895 --> 00:46:52.464
she's able to project herself into
the role of being a French woman.

00:46:53.004 --> 00:46:54.625
It's called method acting.

00:46:55.674 --> 00:46:59.634
Method acting is where you get the
emotion within our, within yourself.

00:46:59.634 --> 00:47:00.245
And then you can.

00:47:00.735 --> 00:47:01.545
But it's not real.

00:47:01.605 --> 00:47:02.445
It's still acting.

00:47:02.595 --> 00:47:03.135
You can do that.

00:47:03.285 --> 00:47:07.155
You can do the accent in a film, et
cetera, real life, something else.

00:47:07.755 --> 00:47:11.775
However, to some extent, speaking
of foreign language is a game.

00:47:12.585 --> 00:47:17.025
To some extent we are imitating the
culture, the language of another

00:47:17.025 --> 00:47:18.765
group, we are pretending to be them.

00:47:18.825 --> 00:47:21.515
We are acting to some extent
or, or you don't agree?

00:47:22.035 --> 00:47:23.280
I think that's fascinating.

00:47:23.770 --> 00:47:24.350
Absolutely.

00:47:25.010 --> 00:47:25.920
Maybe that's

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:26.870
on that not.

00:47:27.095 --> 00:47:28.350
We can, we can end it.

00:47:28.350 --> 00:47:32.370
I think we could probably go on for
hours here, uh, talking about language.

00:47:32.760 --> 00:47:36.580
Uh, I wanna end by, first of all,
I have my, uh, Ukrainian ... on

00:47:36.580 --> 00:47:39.990
today because it's the Ukrainian
national day or day of independence.

00:47:39.990 --> 00:47:44.700
And, uh, I think, uh, we all support
Ukraine and, uh, their heroic

00:47:44.700 --> 00:47:50.819
struggle against this evil invasion,
but, um, also I wanna tell you

00:47:50.819 --> 00:47:52.440
that you have been a big influence.

00:47:53.220 --> 00:47:57.180
Uh, because of your wisdom
and your ability, like when I

00:47:57.180 --> 00:48:01.200
saw that book in Taipei, which
was like this thin, all right.

00:48:01.500 --> 00:48:05.160
And I, I showed the other day in my
video, I got stacks of books that

00:48:05.160 --> 00:48:09.150
are full of complicated language and
everything that they said, and these

00:48:09.150 --> 00:48:11.580
books was reduced into this book.

00:48:11.910 --> 00:48:17.070
And the more we can deal with this instead
of this, the better we can understand,

00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:18.930
you know, the process of language earning.

00:48:18.930 --> 00:48:19.440
So I thank you.

00:48:20.339 --> 00:48:21.270
Well, thank you.

00:48:21.270 --> 00:48:23.129
And thank you for all
the help you've given me.

00:48:23.729 --> 00:48:25.200
And you're still my therapist.

00:48:25.410 --> 00:48:26.609
Appreciate, thank you.

00:48:27.330 --> 00:48:27.879
Thank you.

00:48:27.959 --> 00:48:28.890
No more Prozac.

00:48:29.129 --> 00:48:29.640
Okay.

00:48:29.640 --> 00:48:30.270
Bye Steven.

